course: dictionary
lesson: 543
title: The atom, the smallest piece
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An atom is the smallest piece of a chemical element, such as gold or oxygen. It is far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope, yet everything around us is made of atoms. Look for the clue words in each sentence to work out what they mean.
explain: Atoms join together to make new things. «"H2O" means two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom: that is one molecule of water.» A molecule is a group of atoms joined together, so one water molecule has three atoms in it.
explain: In a chemical change, atoms are rearranged, not lost. «The difference is whether new substances are made: melting and folding only move particles about, but cooking and burning make atoms join up in new ways.» «No atom is lost in a reaction; the atoms are only rearranged, so a rusty nail actually weighs more than the clean one, because it has gained oxygen from the air.»
fact: An atom is the smallest piece of a chemical element.
fact: An atom has a tiny centre called the nucleus, with even tinier electrons moving around it.
fact: «An atom is made of smaller parts: protons and neutrons in a tiny middle called the nucleus, and electrons around it.»
fact: Atoms join together to make molecules, such as water, which is two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.
fact: «Not an atom of» means not even the tiniest bit, as in «not an atom of truth».
exercise: What is an atom? | A kind of liquid that is always hot | A large lump of rock | *The smallest piece of a chemical element, far too small to see
exercise: «H2O» means two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom. What is that called? | *One molecule of water | A periodic table element | A rusty nail
exercise: What does «not an atom of truth» mean? | The whole truth, all of it | Some truth, but not much | *Not even the tiniest bit of truth | A large amount of lies
test: What is at the tiny middle of an atom? | Electrons | *The nucleus | Oxygen | A molecule
test: Why does a rusty nail weigh more than a clean one? | It turns into an atom bomb | It loses atoms to the air | It melts in the rain | *It gains oxygen from the air, and no atom is lost
exam: «Atoms are so small that millions would fit across the full stop at the end of this sentence.» What does this tell you about atoms? | *They are incredibly tiny | They are huge | You can see them with your naked eye | They are the same size as a full stop
